Algorithm: Stack Operations Using Array

-> Initialization:

Step 1: Create an array of given size to store stack elements.
Step 2: Initialize a variable to represent the top position as -1 (indicating empty stack).

-> isEmpty Operation:

Step 1: Check if the top position is -1.
Step 2: If yes, the stack is empty.
Step 3: Otherwise, the stack is not empty.

-> isFull Operation

Step 1: Check if the top position is equal to the last index of the array.
Step 2: If yes, the stack is full.
Step 3: Otherwise, the stack is not full.

-> push Operation

Step 1: Check if the stack is full.
Step 2: If full, do not insert the element.
Step 3: Otherwise, move the top position one step forward.
Step 4: Insert the new element at the top position.

-> pop Operation

Step 1: Check if the stack is empty.
Step 2: If empty, do nothing.
Step 3: Otherwise, move the top position one step backward to remove the top element.

-> peek Operation

Step 1: Check if the stack is empty.
Step 2: If empty, return an invalid value.
Step 3: Otherwise, return the element present at the top position.


Time Complexity: O(1) for all operations
Space Complexity: O(n)



-----> Code <-----


class myStack {
    int arr[];
    int top=-1;
    public myStack(int n) {
        arr=new int[n];
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        if(top==-1)return true;
        return false;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        if(top==arr.length-1)return true;
        return false;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        if(top==arr.length-1)return;
        
        top++;
        arr[top]=x;
    }

    public void pop() {
        if(top==-1)return ;
  
        top--;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if(top==-1)return -1;
        return arr[top];
    }
}
